<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<office:document xmlns:office="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:office:1.0" xmlns:text="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:text:1.0" office:version="1.3" office:mimetype="application/vnd.oasis.opendocument.text">
 <office:body><office:text>
  <text:h text:outline-level="1">Suspected Opioid Overdose: Respond Now</text:h>
  <text:p>Document ID: OOR-HANDOUT-2026-05</text:p>
  <text:p>Approved by: Clinical Governance approval CG-NLX-2026-925</text:p>
  <text:h text:outline-level="2">Recognize</text:h>
  <text:p>Look for unresponsiveness, absent or abnormal breathing, pinpoint pupils, or blue or gray lips and fingertips. If uncertain, treat the situation as an emergency.</text:p>
  <text:h text:outline-level="2">Act</text:h>
  <text:p>1. Call emergency services immediately and follow dispatcher instructions. Do not wait to see whether naloxone works.</text:p>
  <text:p>2. Give naloxone promptly according to the device label and your training.</text:p>
  <text:p>3. Support breathing. Provide rescue breathing or CPR according to your training and dispatcher instructions.</text:p>
  <text:p>4. If the person does not respond, give another available dose every 2 to 3 minutes, using a new device, until the person responds or emergency help takes over.</text:p>
  <text:p>5. Stay with the person. If breathing returns, place them in the recovery position when appropriate and continue monitoring.</text:p>
  <text:h text:outline-level="2">Important warnings</text:h>
  <text:p>Naloxone is temporary and emergency assessment is still required. Symptoms can return. Sudden withdrawal can cause agitation, vomiting, or distress. Do not leave the person alone, give stimulants, place them in cold water, or delay emergency services. Avoid needle-stick exposure and follow local infection-control procedures.</text:p>
 </office:text></office:body>
</office:document>
